1. Overview
This experiment studied the effects variable names had on a programmer's ability to comprehend programs.
Participants completed two programs, one with standard names and one with unrelated names. 
For each program the completion time was recorded. 

2. participants
    - Number of participants: 18
    - Recruitment: Volunteers recruited from the Rochester Institute of Technology's (RITs)
    - Requirements: Volunteers must be RIT students and must have at minimum basic experience programming.
    - Ethics: All data is anonmyous. No data that can uniquely identify the subjects were collected.

3. Materials
Two short programs were created for the experiment:
    - Program 1: A standard program with variable and function names most consider normal
    - Program 2: A program with variable and functions names that were unrelated to their purpose
Both programs were of similar length abd complexity. The subjects never viewed either program before 
starting this experiment.

A survey for subjects to take. Must include:
    - Questions on the quality of the users. Including academic year, current major, and what degree they pursue.
    - A prompt for the user to start a timer.
    - An analysis question asking the subjects what the programs purpose is
    - A question asking the user how long it took to determine the programs purpose.

4. Procedure
    I. Subjects are given a survey that they can complete at their own time
    II. Each subject receives both program 1 and 2 in this order
    III. For each program:
        - The subject starts a time keeping device
        - The subject reviews the program
        - The subject answered a comprehension question
        - The subject records the time taken to complete the task in seconds

5. Data Collection
For each subject the following data was collected:
    - Completion time for program 1
    - Answer to the program 1 comprehension question
    - Completion time for program 2
    - Answer to the program 2 comprehension question
No information is to be collected that can uniquely identify subjects

6. Data Processing
    - Answers were checked and would receive a rating from 1 to 10.
    - Any participants who's answers scored below a 7 were removed from the data
    - Completion times were used directly without transformation

7. Statistical Analysis
A paired samples t-test method was used to compare completion times between Programs 1 and 2.

Analysis steps:
    I. Compute difference score between the programs times
    II. Calculate standard deviation and standard error using the difference score mean
    III. Apply paired-samples t-test formula
    IV. Report t-value, degrees of freedom, p-value

8. Reproducibility
    I. Open data/raw_data.csv
    II. Compute difference score between the programs times
    III. Calculate standard deviation and standard error using the difference score mean
    III. Run paired t-test
    IV. Compare results with results/summary_statistics

9. Limitations
- Small sample size
- Programs subjects reviewed may have been too simple and cannot be generalized
    to large more complex programs

